;; A network interface over the USB cable, for boards with no Ethernet.
;;
;; The Raspberry Pi Zero has two micro-B ports and neither is an Ethernet
;; jack. The one marked USB can run as a peripheral instead of a host, so
;; the board can present itself to a computer as a network adapter: one
;; cable for power and network, nothing else to buy.
;;
;; Three things have to line up, and they live in three different places,
;; which is why this is a feature rather than a note in a README:
;;
;;   config.txt   dtoverlay=dwc2, or the port stays a host port
;;   kernel       dwc2 and g_ether, which bcmrpi builds as modules
;;   userspace    load them and give usb0 an address
;;
;; The addresses are static on both ends: 192.168.7.1 on the board,
;; 192.168.7.2 on the computer. A DHCP server for a two-node link would be
;; more machinery than the link.
;;
;; The config.txt is the one part this fragment does not state. Every
;; Raspberry Pi target already names one, and two fragments naming
;; different files for one symbol is a conflict silt refuses rather than
;; resolving by order - there is no sensible rule for which firmware config
;; a board should boot with. So the image says, with an override, and this
;; pack ships a config.txt for the Zero with the dwc2 line added:
;;
;;   (override
;;     (value buildroot:BR2_PACKAGE_RPI_FIRMWARE_CONFIG_FILE
;;            "$(BR2_EXTERNAL_USB_GADGET_PATH)/files/config.txt"))

(fragment feature:usb-gadget-net

  (doc "usb0 network over the board's own USB cable, 192.168.7.1")

  (requires
    (capability mmu))

  (buildroot
    ;; /etc/init.d/S39usbnet, before anything that wants a network.
    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "overlay"))

  (linux
    ;; The controller and the gadget. bcmrpi has dwc2 as a module and the
    ;; gadget framework built in; g_ether is the legacy Ethernet gadget,
    ;; which is far simpler to bring up than the configfs route.
    (y CONFIG_USB_GADGET)
    (m CONFIG_USB_DWC2)
    (m CONFIG_USB_ETH)))
